{
 "duration": 47.8,
 "captions": [
  {
   "start": 0.0,
   "text": "Your water heater is a hidden water tank.",
   "end": 2.58
  },
  {
   "start": 2.58,
   "text": "The CDC says so.",
   "end": 4.15
  },
  {
   "start": 4.15,
   "text": "But some homes have two tanks, and you should never drink from one.",
   "end": 8.08
  },
  {
   "start": 8.08,
   "text": "If your heater has a tank, it feeds your hot faucet and shower.",
   "end": 12.11
  },
  {
   "start": 12.11,
   "text": "Out of bottled water?",
   "end": 13.29
  },
  {
   "start": 13.29,
   "text": "The EPA says you may be able to drain your hot water tank, or even your pipes.",
   "end": 18.14
  },
  {
   "start": 18.14,
   "text": "Careful, though.",
   "end": 19.0
  },
  {
   "start": 19.0,
   "text": "Some older homes have a second tank for the radiators.",
   "end": 22.17
  },
  {
   "start": 22.17,
   "text": "The CDC says never drink, cook or wash with radiator or boiler water.",
   "end": 27.52
  },
  {
   "start": 27.52,
   "text": "This water could make you sick.",
   "end": 29.33
  },
  {
   "start": 29.33,
   "text": "So use the tank that feeds your faucets, not the heating tank.",
   "end": 33.03
  },
  {
   "start": 33.03,
   "text": "To get the water out, follow the drain and safety steps in your heater's manual.",
   "end": 37.42
  },
  {
   "start": 37.42,
   "text": "Read it today, while the water still runs.",
   "end": 39.91
  },
  {
   "start": 39.91,
   "text": "Before you drink that water, boil it or treat it, every time.",
   "end": 43.94
  },
  {
   "start": 43.94,
   "text": "The EPA says that goes for any source.",
   "end": 46.62
  },
  {
   "start": 46.62,
   "text": "And remember this.",
   "end": 47.8
  }
 ],
